From: zenmode-adri <274425265+zenmode-adri@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: rk817-charger: displayed soc with full/empty anchors

The driver published the raw coulomb counter as the percentage and a
periodic "ocv recalibration" overwrote the counter from the device tree
ocv table. Those tables put 100 % near 4.05 V while packs charge to
4.2 V, and are copied between boards without being measured, so the
percentage jumped (95 -> 100 while discharging) and drifted for days.
On boards where the charger is an external IC the PMIC never reports
CHARGE_FINISH, so the full anchor and the capacity learning never ran.

Move the decision to rk817_gauge.h, a header-only, integer-only logic
without kernel dependencies (the same file is exercised by a host
simulator):

 - the displayed soc follows the counter without moving against the
   charge direction, by at most 1 % per 8 s pass, and stays at 99 %
   until a full anchor (PMIC FINISH, or a tail the logic recognises on
   chargers that cut off at once or never report it);
 - an empty anchor, compensated for the load, rewrites the counter to 0
   and a low zone pulls an optimistic counter down as the pack empties;
 - fcc is learned between clean full/empty anchors, bounded;
 - the ocv table is only used for a trusted rest after a long suspend,
   and may only lower the counter.

The driver now reads the raw signed counter, feeds the gauge once per
pass under a mutex (work, plug ISRs and resume all come here), applies
its decision (counter rewrite with 1 uAh precision, NVRAM, capacity,
status and online) and notifies userspace only when one of those
changed. An optional dc-det-gpios property of the charger node is
accepted as plug detection; without it nothing changes. The sleep
credit for a charge that happened while suspended is kept, as a
correction of the counter.

---
